Here is an example of a JavaScript function that generates a secure password of length at least 10 characters with the specified requirements:
```javascript
function generatePassword() {
  const lowercase = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz';
  const uppercase = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';
  const numbers = '0123456789';
  const symbols = '!@#$%^&*()_+-={}[]|\\:;"<>,.?/~`';
  const allChars = lowercase + uppercase + numbers + symbols;
  let password = '';
  let uniqueChars = '';
  // Generate password with at least one uppercase, lowercase, number, and symbol
  password += getRandomChar(lowercase);
  password += getRandomChar(uppercase);
  password += getRandomChar(numbers);
  password += getRandomChar(symbols);
  // Generate remaining password characters
  for (let i = 4; i < 10; i++) {
    password += getRandomChar(allChars);
  }
  // Check for minimum number of unique characters
  for (let char of password) {
    if (!uniqueChars.includes(char)) {
      uniqueChars += char;
    }
  }
  if (uniqueChars.length < 8) {
    return generatePassword();
  }
  // Check against common passwords
  const commonPasswords = ['password', '123456', 'qwerty', 'abc123', 'letmein'];
  if (commonPasswords.includes(password)) {
    return generatePassword();
  }
  return password;
}
function getRandomChar(chars) {
  const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * chars.length);
  return chars[randomIndex];
}
```
This function first defines four strings containing the lowercase letters, uppercase letters, numbers, and symbols that can be used in the password. It then combines these strings into a single `allChars` string that contains all possible characters that can be used in the password.
The function then generates the first four characters of the password by selecting one random character from each of the four character sets. It then generates the remaining six characters of the password by selecting random characters from the `allChars` string.
After generating the password, the function checks whether it contains at least eight unique characters. If not, it generates a new password until this requirement is met.
Finally, the function checks the generated password against a list of common passwords and rejects it if any matches are found.

