Sure, here's a Java code implementation of the law of supply and demand:
```java
public class SupplyAndDemand {
    private double demandSlope; // slope of demand curve
    private double demandIntercept; // intercept of demand curve
    private double supplySlope; // slope of supply curve
    private double supplyIntercept; // intercept of supply curve
    // constructor to initialize the slopes and intercepts of the demand and supply curves
    public SupplyAndDemand(double demandSlope, double demandIntercept, double supplySlope, double supplyIntercept) {
        this.demandSlope = demandSlope;
        this.demandIntercept = demandIntercept;
        this.supplySlope = supplySlope;
        this.supplyIntercept = supplyIntercept;
    }
    // method to calculate quantity demanded based on the price
    public double calculateQuantityDemanded(double price) {
        return demandIntercept - demandSlope * price;
    }
    // method to calculate quantity supplied based on the price
    public double calculateQuantitySupplied(double price) {
        return supplySlope * price + supplyIntercept;
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `SupplyAndDemand` class has four instance variables representing the slopes and intercepts of the demand and supply curves. The constructor initializes these values. The `calculateQuantityDemanded` method takes in a price and uses the demand curve equation (Qd = a - bP) to calculate the corresponding quantity demanded. The `calculateQuantitySupplied` method takes in a price and uses the supply curve equation (Qs = c + dP) to calculate the corresponding quantity supplied.

