```java
public class PrimeSumCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {11, 14, 17, 19, 22, 24, 27, 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 39};
        int sum = sumOfPrimes(arr);
        System.out.println(sum);
    }

    public static int sumOfPrimes(int[] arr) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (isPrime(arr[i])) {
                sum += arr[i];
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
The `sumOfPrimes` method iterates through the input array and uses the `isPrime` helper method to determine if each number is prime or not. If a number is prime, it is added to the `sum` variable. The `isPrime` method uses a simple algorithm to check if a number is prime or not - it returns `false` if the number is less than or equal to 1, and iterates from 2 to the square root of the number checking if it is divisible by any number in that range. If it is, the method returns `false`, otherwise it returns `true`.
