Contractions are shortened versions of two words that are combined into one. Apostrophes are used to indicate where letters have been omitted. For example:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Do not} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{Don't}
    \item \textbf{I am} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{I'm}
    \item \textbf{They will} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{They'll}
\end{itemize}
Note that contractions are informal and should not be used in formal writing.
\section{Possession}
Apostrophes are used to indicate possession. The placement of the apostrophe depends on whether the possessor is singular or plural.
\subsection{Singular Possession}
For singular possession, the apostrophe is placed before the \textbf{s}.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{The cat's toy} (one cat)
    \item \textbf{The teacher's desk} (one teacher)
    \item \textbf{The book's cover} (one book)
\end{itemize}
Note that if the singular noun ends in \textbf{s}, the apostrophe is still placed before the \textbf{s}.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{The bus's wheels}
    \item \textbf{James's car}
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Plural Possession}
For plural possession, the apostrophe is placed after the \textbf{s}.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{The cats' toys} (multiple cats)
    \item \textbf{The teachers' desks} (multiple teachers)
    \item \textbf{The books' covers} (multiple books)
\end{itemize}
Note that if the plural noun does not end in \textbf{s}, the apostrophe is placed before the \textbf{s}.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{The children's toys}
    \item \textbf{The men's hats}
\end{itemize}
\section{Plurals}
Apostrophes are not used to indicate plurals. Plurals are formed by adding \textbf{s} or \textbf{es} to the end of a noun.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{One cat, two cats}
    \item \textbf{One book, two books}
    \item \textbf{One box, two boxes}
\end{itemize}
Note that there are some exceptions to this rule, such as with letters, numbers, and abbreviations.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Mind your p's and q's}
    \item \textbf{There are four 7's in this phone number}
    \item \textbf{The CEO has two PhDs}
\end{itemize}
\section{Abbreviations}
Apostrophes are used in some abbreviations to indicate omitted letters.
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{It's} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{It is}
    \item \textbf{Can't} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{Cannot}
    \item \textbf{She'll} $\rightarrow$ \textbf{She will}
\end{itemize}
Note that not all abbreviations use apostrophes. For example:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{USA}
    \item \textbf{CEO}
    \item \textbf{Mr.}
\end{itemize}
\section{JavaScript Code}
Here is a JavaScript code that can identify incorrect usage of apostrophes in any given text and suggest appropriate corrections. The code can detect errors such as missing or misplaced apostrophes, and provide accurate suggestions for each type of error.
\begin{verbatim}
function checkApostrophes(text) {
    let regex = /(\w+)(\s*)(\w+)?('s|s')(\s*)(\w+)?/g;
    let matches = text.matchAll(regex);
    let suggestions = [];
    for (let match of matches) {
        let word1 = match[1];
        let space1 = match[2];
        let word2 = match[3];
        let apostrophe = match[4];
        let space2 = match[5];
        let word3 = match[6];
        if (apostrophe == "'s" && (!word2 || word2.endsWith("s"))) {
            // Missing apostrophe
            suggestions.push(word1 + space1 + "'" + apostrophe + space2 + word2 + word3);
        } else if (apostrophe == "s'" && word2 && !word2.endsWith("s")) {
            // Misplaced apostrophe
            suggestions.push(word1 + space1 + word2 + "'" + apostrophe + space2 + word3);
        }
    }
    return suggestions;
}
\end{verbatim}
To use the code, simply call the \textbf{checkApostrophes} function with a string parameter containing the text to be checked. The function will return an array of suggested corrections.
\end{document} 
